so w(2753) = 4165.
Remark 2.8. On any family F , wi and w are invertible. Given f ∈ F, we get wi−1 (f ) by repeatedly
subtracting 1 from the value of f (i) until we get a function in F .
  We now discuss orbits and homomesy for w. Theorem 2.10 is the main result of this section. We will
prove the different cases of this theorem in the upcoming subsections 2.3, 2.4, and 2.5.
Definition 2.9. For j ∈ [k], define ηj (f ) = #f −1 ({j}) to be the number of times j appears as an output
of the function f .
Theorem 2.10. Fix F to be either Injm (n, k) or Sur1 (n, k) for positive integers n, k, m. Then under the
action of w on F, ηj is nk -mesic for any j ∈ [k].
            P
   Note that j ηj (f ) = n for all f . It is clear that an equivalent statement to Theorem 2.10 is that ηi − ηj
is 0-mesic for any i, j ∈ [k]. That is, i and j appear as outputs of functions the same number of times
across any orbit. We also conjecture this result for m-surjections in general.
Conjecture 2.11. Let F = Surm (n, k) for positive integers m, n, k. Then under the action of w on F,
ηj is nk -mesic for any j ∈ [k].
   Conjecture 2.11 has been verified by a computer for all triples (m, n, k) where n ≤ 10 (for which there
are finitely many relevant triples since Surm (n, k) = ∅ if mk > n).

2.2    Orbit board terminology and notation
In this subsection, we introduce terminology and notation that applies to whirling on any family of func-
tions F from [n] to [k] that we will use on various homomesy proofs. We use a generalization of the tuple
boards of Haddadan (2021).
   Let O be an orbit under the action of w on F. We draw a board for the orbit O by placing some f ∈ O
on the top row. The function in row i + 1 is wi (f ) for i ∈ [0, ℓ(O) − 1], where ℓ(O) is the length of O.
2.3    The proof of Theorem 2.10 for injections
In this subsection, we prove Theorem 2.10 when F = Inj1 (n, k). Even though this is a special case of
m-injections, the proof here is much simpler.
Lemma 2.15. Suppose i is in position P of a board for the w-orbit O on Inj1 (n, k).
   1. There is exactly one occurrence of i + 1 within (P, [1, n]).
   2. There is exactly one occurrence of i − 1 within (P, [−n, −1]).
6                                                                                               Michael Joseph et al.

Proof: To prove (1), suppose position P is in column j. So f (j) = i for some f ∈ O. Then (P, [1, n])
contains the multiset of outputs of (wj ◦ · · · ◦ w1 )(f ) in some order. If (wj−1 ◦ · · · ◦ w1 )(f ) does not have
i + 1 already as an output, then by definition wj changes the output corresponding to the input j from i
to i + 1. So there is an occurrence of i + 1 within (P, [1, n]). Since (wj ◦ · · · ◦ w1 )(f ) cannot have any
output more than once, this occurrence is unique.
   The proof of (2) is analogous to (1) using the inverse of whirling instead.

Proof of Theorem 2.10 for F = Inj1 (n, k): By Lemma 2.15, we have a bijection between i-entries and
(i + 1)-entries for any i ∈ [k − 1], leading immediately to the equidistribution.

